  "title": "Queueing theory for backend capacity",
  "content": "capacity planning was never the hard part. Knowing when it was about to fail was. Here's what changes once the guessing is replaced with measurement. It only shows up under the exact conditions nobody tests for.\n\nYou don't need a PhD in queueing theory to avoid the classic capacity planning mistake. You need one formula and the discipline to use it before your p99 latency page goes off at 3am.\n\nThe scenario is familiar: a service that handles 200 requests per second at 40% CPU utilisation, with a p99 latency of 80ms. The team adds a feature that doubles traffic to 400 rps. They provision twice the CPU, expecting the same 80ms p99. Instead, p99 jumps to 400ms, and the on-call engineer spends the night adding instances while the load balancer sheds connections. The root cause isn't a bug in the new feature; it's that utilisation doubled from 0.4 to 0.8, and at 0.8 utilisation, queueing delay is not twice as bad—it's roughly four times worse. That nonlinearity is the entire point of queueing theory, and it's the part that capacity spreadsheets miss.\n\nThis post walks through the mechanism, shows the arithmetic with a worked example, and gives you a monitoring and provisioning process you can apply to any request-response service. The goal is not to turn you into a queueing theorist; it's to give you a mental model that turns capacity planning from guesswork into a calculation you can defend in a design review.\n\n## The utilisation cliff: why 80% is not 80%\n\nThe core result comes from the M/M/1 queue—the simplest model of a single server with random arrivals and random service times. The average time a request spends in the system (waiting plus being served) is:\n\nW = 1 / (μ - λ)\n\nwhere λ is the arrival rate (requests per second) and μ is the service rate (requests per second the server can handle). Utilisation ρ = λ / μ. Rewriting in terms of ρ and service time S = 1/μ:\n\nW = S / (1 - ρ)\n\nWhen ρ = 0.4, W = S / 0.6 ≈ 1.67 S. When ρ = 0.8, W = S / 0.2 = 5 S. So doubling utilisation from 0.4 to 0.8 multiplies the average time in system by 3—not 2. And that's for the average; the tail is worse.\n\nThe p99 latency in an M/M/1 queue scales even more sharply. For an exponential service time distribution, the 99th percentile of time in system is approximately S * ln(100) / (1 - ρ). At ρ = 0.4, that's about 4.6 S / 0.6 ≈ 7.7 S. At ρ = 0.8, it's 4.6 S / 0.2 = 23 S. So the p99 grows by a factor of 3 when utilisation doubles—the same factor as the mean in this model, but the absolute numbers are much larger relative to service time.\n\nReal services are not M/M/1. Service times are not exponential; they have lower variance. Arrivals are not Poisson; they burst. But the qualitative shape—latency rising hyperbolically as utilisation approaches 1—holds for any queue with variability. The M/M/1 formula is a conservative approximation that gives you the right intuition and a safety margin.\n\nThe practical implication: you cannot plan capacity by linear extrapolation. If you're at 40% utilisation and traffic doubles, you don't need 80% of your current capacity—you need enough headroom to keep utilisation below a threshold where latency is acceptable. For most interactive services, that threshold is somewhere between 60% and 70% for CPU, and lower for resources with higher variance like database connections or thread pools.\n\n## Where the capacity spreadsheet goes wrong\n\nA typical capacity planning exercise looks like this: measure current CPU at peak, measure requests per second at peak, compute CPU per request, multiply by forecasted requests, add 20% headroom, file a ticket for more instances. This approach fails for three reasons.\n\nFirst, it ignores the queueing nonlinearity described above. Adding 20% headroom on top of 80% utilisation gives you 96% utilisation after the traffic increase—a latency disaster. The correct headroom calculation uses the latency target, not a fixed percentage.\n\nSecond, it treats CPU as the only bottleneck. In a typical web backend, the bottleneck is often not CPU but a downstream dependency: a database connection pool, a thread pool in an application server, a connection limit on a load balancer, or a rate limit on an external API. Each of these is a queue with its own service rate and its own utilisation curve. The one with the highest utilisation relative to its latency target is your bottleneck, and adding CPU won't help.\n\nThird, it uses averages. Capacity planning based on average requests per second misses the peaks. A service that averages 200 rps but peaks at 600 rps every day at noon needs to be provisioned for the peak, not the average. And the peak itself has a distribution—the highest peak of the month is higher than the highest peak of the day.\n\nThe fix is to model each constrained resource as a queue, measure its utilisation at peak, and compute the latency at the forecasted utilisation using a queueing approximation. The resource with the worst projected latency is your scaling priority.\n\n## A worked example: from 200 to 400 rps\n\nAssume a service with the following measured characteristics at peak (these are illustrative numbers, not a benchmark):\n\n- Arrival rate: 200 requests per second\n- Average service time per request on one instance: 10ms of CPU time\n- Instance count: 4\n- CPU utilisation per instance: 0.5 (so each instance handles 50 rps, and 50 rps * 10ms = 0.5 CPU-seconds per second)\n\nThe service time S = 10ms. Utilisation ρ = 0.5. Using the M/M/1 approximation, average time in system W = S / (1 - ρ) = 10ms / 0.5 = 20ms. The p99 is roughly S * ln(100) / (1 - ρ) = 10ms * 4.6 / 0.5 = 92ms. That matches the observed p99 of ~90ms.\n\nNow traffic doubles to 400 rps. If we keep 4 instances, each handles 100 rps, so utilisation per instance becomes 1.0—the system is saturated and queues grow without bound. If we double to 8 instances, utilisation stays at 0.5 and latency stays the same. But what if we add only 6 instances? Utilisation becomes 400 / (6 * 100) = 0.667. Then W = 10ms / 0.333 = 30ms, and p99 ≈ 10ms * 4.6 / 0.333 = 138ms. That's a 50% increase in p99 for a 33% increase in capacity—not linear.\n\nIf the SLO is p99 < 150ms, 6 instances are acceptable. If the SLO is p99 < 100ms, you need 8 instances (or a reduction in service time). The decision is now a calculation, not a guess.\n\nBut wait—the M/M/1 model assumes one server. In reality, you have multiple instances behind a load balancer, which is an M/M/c queue. The formula for M/M/c is more complex, but the key difference is that a pool of servers is more efficient than a single server of equivalent total capacity. For c servers each with service rate μ, the utilisation per server is ρ = λ / (c μ), and the probability of queueing is lower than for a single server at the same ρ. The Erlang C formula gives the exact queueing probability, and the average wait time is:\n\nW_q = C(c, ρ) * S / (c (1 - ρ))\n\nwhere C(c, ρ) is the Erlang C probability. For planning purposes, you can use the approximation that a pool of c servers behaves like a single server with service rate c μ but with a correction factor that reduces the wait time. In practice, for c >= 4 and ρ <= 0.8, the M/M/1 approximation is conservative (it overestimates latency), which is fine for capacity planning—you'd rather over-provision slightly than under-provision.\n\n## The bottleneck you didn't measure: connection pools and thread pools\n\nCPU is rarely the first resource to saturate in a typical backend. More often, it's a fixed-size pool: a database connection pool (e.g., HikariCP in Java, with a default maximum of 10 connections), a thread pool in a servlet container (e.g., Tomcat's default maxThreads of 200), or an HTTP client connection pool. These are queues with a hard limit on the number of servers (connections or threads), and when they saturate, requests wait indefinitely or fail fast.\n\nThe queueing model for a fixed pool is M/M/c/c—a system with c servers and no queue (requests that arrive when all servers are busy are dropped). The probability that an arriving request finds all servers busy is given by the Erlang B formula. For a pool of 10 connections, each holding a connection for an average of 5ms, the maximum throughput is 10 / 0.005 = 2000 requests per second. At 1500 rps, utilisation is 0.75, and the Erlang B blocking probability is roughly 0.1—10% of requests would be rejected if there were no queue. In practice, there is a queue (the request waits for a connection), so the wait time grows as utilisation approaches 1.\n\nThe key point: a connection pool of size 10 is a queue with 10 servers. If your service handles 200 rps and each request holds a connection for 5ms, the utilisation is 200 * 0.005 / 10 = 0.1—fine. If traffic grows to 1500 rps, utilisation becomes 0.75, and the wait for a connection becomes significant. Adding CPU instances won't help if the database can't handle more connections; you need to either increase the pool size (if the database allows) or reduce the connection hold time.\n\nA common failure mode is a connection pool that is sized for average load but not peak load. The pool saturates during a traffic spike, requests queue for connections, and latency spikes even though CPU is idle. The fix is to measure the pool utilisation (active connections / max connections) at peak and apply the same queueing calculation: keep utilisation below 0.7 at peak to avoid queueing delay.\n\n## Monitoring: the metrics that tell you you're near the cliff\n\nYou can't manage what you don't measure, and the metrics that matter for queueing are not the ones most dashboards show. CPU utilisation is necessary but not sufficient. You need to measure utilisation of every constrained resource, and you need to measure latency at the tail, not just the average.\n\nThe metrics to track:\n\n- **Utilisation per resource**: CPU per instance, database connection pool active/max, thread pool active/max, load balancer active connections / max, queue depth for any internal queues (e.g., a work queue between a web tier and a worker tier).\n- **Latency percentiles**: p50, p95, p99, and p99.9 for each service and each downstream dependency. The p99 is your early warning; the p99.9 is your canary for the cliff.\n- **Arrival rate and service time**: requests per second and average time per request, measured at the same point. From these you can compute utilisation and compare it to the measured utilisation to validate your model.\n- **Queue depth and wait time**: for any explicit queue (e.g., a message queue or a thread pool queue), measure the number of items waiting and the time they wait. A rising queue depth at constant arrival rate is a sign that service time is increasing—often due to a downstream bottleneck.\n\nThe pattern to watch for: as utilisation crosses 0.7, p99 starts to grow faster than p50. That's the queueing effect. If you see p99 growing while p50 is stable, you're approaching the cliff. The fix is to add capacity or reduce service time before utilisation hits 0.8.\n\nA useful dashboard is a scatter plot of utilisation vs. p99 latency for each resource, with a fitted curve. The curve should follow the 1/(1-ρ) shape. If it deviates upward, there's a bottleneck you haven't modeled—perhaps a lock contention or a garbage collection pause.\n\n## A minimal queueing calculator in Python\n\nYou can compute the M/M/1 and M/M/c latency estimates with a few lines of Python. This is not a replacement for measurement, but it's useful for quick what-if analysis during design reviews.\n\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef mm1_latency(service_time_ms, utilisation):\n    \"\"\"Average time in system for M/M/1, in ms.\"\"\"\n    if utilisation >= 1:\n        return float('inf')\n    return service_time_ms / (1 - utilisation)\n\ndef mm1_p99(service_time_ms, utilisation):\n    \"\"\"Approximate p99 for M/M/1 with exponential service times.\"\"\"\n    if utilisation >= 1:\n        return float('inf')\n    return service_time_ms * math.log(100) / (1 - utilisation)\n\ndef erlang_c(c, utilisation):\n    \"\"\"Erlang C probability for M/M/c queue.\"\"\"\n    if utilisation >= 1:\n        return 1.0\n    numerator = (c * utilisation) ** c / math.factorial(c) * (1 / (1 - utilisation))\n    sum_terms = sum((c * utilisation) ** k / math.factorial(k) for k in range(c))\n    return numerator / (sum_terms + numerator)\n\ndef mmc_latency(service_time_ms, c, arrival_rate):\n    \"\"\"Average time in system for M/M/c, in ms.\"\"\"\n    mu = 1 / (service_time_ms / 1000)  # service rate per server, per second\n    rho = arrival_rate / (c * mu)\n    if rho >= 1:\n        return float('inf')\n    wait = erlang_c(c, rho) * (service_time_ms / 1000) / (c * (1 - rho))\n    return (wait + service_time_ms / 1000) * 1000\n\n# Example: service time 10ms, 4 instances, arrival rate 200 rps\nprint(mmc_latency(10, 4, 200))  # ~20ms\nprint(mmc_latency(10, 4, 400))  # inf (saturated)\nprint(mmc_latency(10, 8, 400))  # ~20ms\nprint(mmc_latency(10, 6, 400))  # ~30ms\n```\n\nRun this with your own service time and instance count to see how latency scales with traffic. The numbers will be approximate because real service times are not exponential and arrivals are not Poisson, but the shape is right.\n\n## The broader lesson: capacity is a distribution, not a number\n\nThe deeper principle is that capacity is not a single number you can provision to; it's a relationship between arrival rate, service time, and the tail of the latency distribution. Any capacity plan that says \"we need N instances\" without specifying the latency target and the utilisation threshold is incomplete. The right question is: \"At what utilisation does our p99 exceed the SLO?\" And the answer comes from measurement plus a queueing model, not from a spreadsheet that multiplies averages.\n\nThis also changes how you think about autoscaling. A reactive autoscaler that adds instances when CPU exceeds 70% is already too late—by the time CPU hits 70%, you're on the steep part of the curve, and the instances take time to boot and warm up. A better approach is to scale on a leading indicator: request rate relative to capacity, or queue depth, or p99 latency relative to SLO. And to provision a buffer so that normal traffic spikes don't cross the cliff.\n\nThe same reasoning applies to databases, message queues, and any shared resource. Each is a queue. Each has a utilisation curve. The one with the highest utilisation relative to its latency target is your constraint. Find it, measure it, and plan around it.\n\n## Start here: compute your current utilisation and p99\n\nThe next step is concrete: pick one service you own, and for its busiest hour, pull the requests per second and the average service time (or CPU time per request) from your metrics. Compute utilisation per instance and compare it to the p99 latency you observe. If p99 is more than 3x the average service time, you're likely above 0.6 utilisation and should plan to add capacity or reduce service time before the next traffic increase. Do this for every constrained resource—CPU, connection pools, thread pools—and you'll have a capacity model you can defend.\n\n## Official documentation\n\n- [Google SRE Book: Monitoring Distributed Systems](https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/)\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** November 2026\n",
